#### Oct 27, 2026 | Tejesh Vaish

# Check an agent's browser task against the page with Taiko

`Approve order` can return without an error and still leave the browser on the wrong page. In this local example, the browser lands on `/session-expired`. The page says `Order 1001 approved`, but it has none of the order's result markers.

Define the result before clicking, then check it after the action. For this task, the expected URL and the order's ID, status, and confirmation must all match. This check covers the demo's DOM state. To verify that a real approval was saved, you also need evidence from the system that stores it.

A fixed script stands in for an agent and always reports `completed`. No LLM or production integration is involved. The complete [pinned example](https://github.com/tridev-tej/technical-blog-examples/tree/005c62af00ce3cac768dc6e1e5d551fcc246859a/taiko-agent-task) contains the local site and `workflow.test.cjs` runner.

## Read the result with Taiko

The runner uses Taiko's [button selector](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/button/) and [click](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/click/) to attempt `Approve order`. After the attempt, [currentURL](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/currenturl/) reads the browser's location and [evaluate](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/evaluate/) runs `readPage` to collect the result markers. The callback reads the page without adding markers or changing the order.

`readPage` collects `[data-order-id]`, `[data-status]`, and `[data-confirmation]`. It records each element's trimmed `innerText` and a visibility flag. The acceptance rule requires exactly one of each marker with the expected value. Duplicate matching markers are rejected.

This excerpt checks those values after the action. The complete runner supplies `readPage` and the browser setup:

~~~javascript
function oneVisibleValue(values, expected) {
  return values.length === 1 &&
    values[0].visible && values[0].text === expected;
}

function isCompleted(observed, expectedURL, input) {
  return observed.url === expectedURL &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.order, input.orderId) &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.status, 'Approved') &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.confirmation,
      `Order ${input.orderId} approved`);
}
~~~

`expectedURL` comes from the task's starting URL, and `input.orderId` is `1001`. `isCompleted` doesn't use the script's completion report or the selected scenario to decide the result.

The visibility flag is approximate. It checks box dimensions, intersection with the visible browser area, and ancestor styles. It doesn't prove that an element is unobscured or accessible.

The URL, DOM, and button count are read one after another, so the observation isn't atomic: the page can change between readings. The demo updates its DOM synchronously. If your application updates asynchronously, decide when its result has settled enough to read.

