The expired page puts the approval phrase in an ordinary paragraph. A body-text search accepts it because the words are present, even though there's no order result. Checking the URL and the specific result markers rejects that false positive.

The runner also covers a missing button: the click throws and the order stays `Pending`. A fourth test demonstrates the phrase search's false positive on the expired page. Two recorded runs each passed all four cases, with no failures or skips.

This example doesn't cover authentication, payments, production workflows, frames, shadow DOM, delayed updates, or connection recovery.

## Try the four cases

Use the pinned revision linked above and keep all five files in `taiko-agent-task` together, including the unchanged lockfile. The reader requires Linux; the recorded runs used Node 22.23.1 and Taiko 1.5.0. Other platforms and versions haven't been tested.

Before running it, unset every `TAIKO_*` variable, plus `LOCAL_PROTOCOL`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, and `NODE_PATH`; the runner rejects these overrides. From `taiko-agent-task`, run `npm ci` with install scripts enabled to download the bundled browser; this may use the network. Create new writable XDG config and cache directories, then run `npm test` with `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_CACHE_HOME` set to them.

Each run writes an `evidence-*` directory. Compare `success.json` with `unexpected-navigation.json`: each records the action outcome and the page result. `text-only-control.json` records the expected assertion failure from the phrase search. The PNGs show the final pages.

For your own task, choose its expected URL and result values before writing the action. Include a test page with the success words but no valid result markers, and check that it is rejected.
